$\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ from the back side of the photoanode. The photoelectrode turned dark after a few cycles as shown in Figure 4a. $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ -(B) was deposited using the same number of cycles (50 cycles) but in a narrower potential window between 1.0 and $1.2{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs. RHE. The $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ -(C) catalyst was deposited in the potential window between 1.0 and $1.2{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs. RHE but at pH 5.5.
XPS spectra of the $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ catalysts on hematite are similar to those of typical NiOOH-type catalysts (Figure S7). HR-SEM images of the hematite flat films before and after deposition of the $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ catalysts are shown in Figure 4a. The $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ -(A) and $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ -(B) catalysts are visible as nanosheets, and the sheets are smaller in size for $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ -(B), indicating an influence by the deposition potential window. A similar observation was earlier made in the electrodeposition of $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ from a solution of nickel acetate, where the oxygen evolution at higher potentials was found to hinder the nucleation rate of $\mathrm{Ni}\bar{\mathrm{O}}_{\mathrm{x}}$ .36 The $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ -(C) catalyst has a lower loading, is thinner, and allows a higher transmittance of light than the same material deposited at higher pH (Figure 4b).
Cyclic voltammetry under illumination revealed a $\mathrm{Ni(OH)}_{2}\mathrm{~/~}$ 1 NiOOH couple at $0.79{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs. RHE at $\mathsf{p H}13.6$ (Figure S8). In the dark, the $\mathrm{Ni(OH)_{2}/N i O O H}$ couple on hematite was observed at $1.3{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs. RHE, which is the same as on FTO. The photovoltage generated by the hematite flat film can be estimated from the shift of the potential of the $\mathrm{Ni(OH)_{2}/N i O O H}$ couple under illumination. Thus, the photovoltage is about 0.5 V. This value is in agreement with the open-circuit potential measurements of $\mathrm{NiO_{x}/F e_{2}O_{3}}$ photoelectrodes (Figure S9).
The performance of the three $\mathrm{NiO_{x}/F e_{2}O_{3}}$ photoanodes in PEC oxygen evolution is compared in Figure $\scriptstyle4\ c$ . The $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}.$ (C) catalyst absorbs only a small portion of light (Figure 4a and 4b). Thus, front illumination was carried out. An improvement in the onset potential (defined by Le Formal et al. as the potential at which the derivative of the photocurrentpotential curve becomes greater than $0.2\mathrm{\mA\cm^{\tilde{-}2}V^{-1}},$ ) by 130 $\mathrm{mV}$ and an increase in photocurrent compared to bare hematite film was observed. The $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ -(A) and $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ -(B) catalysts absorb significantly the light during front illumination (Figure 4a and 4b) and reduce the light intensity reaching the hematite, lowering the photocurrents at higher bias potential (Figure 4c). Illumination was then carried out from the back side. The Ni$\mathrm{{O}_{x}}$ catalysts gave an average improvement of about $120\mathrm{mV}$ on the onset potential (Figure S10).
While onset potential is commonly used as a metric to characterize the PEC OER, this potential should be determined with caution due to the possible interference of non-catalytic oxidation of catalysts before real OER current begins. Figure S11a shows the difference in the observed onset potential for $\mathrm{NiO_{x}/F e_{2}O_{3}}$ at different scan rates. When scanned from $0.4\mathrm{~V~}$ at a scan rate of $10\mathrm{~mV~s^{-1}}$ , an apparent onset potential for water oxidation was observed at $0.6\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE. When scanned at $\boldsymbol{1}\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ by cyclic voltammetry, the onset potential was observed at $0.85\mathrm{~V~}$ vs RHE. The oxidation current at 0.6 V observed at faster scan rate is in fact due to the oxidation of catalyst from $\mathrm{{Ni}(I I)}$ to Ni(III). Scanning in both directions by cyclic voltammetry should reveal the redox behavior of a catalyst on the surface of the photoelectrode even when using faster scan rates (Figure S11a) and allow the differentiation of catalyst oxidation from PEC water oxidation at low applied bias. In the literature, PEC OER is commonly carried out with a scan rate of $10\mathrm{~mV~s~}^{-1}$ or greater.  Verification of reported onset potentials under those conditions is out of the scope of this study. For a more meaningful comparison of selected OER catalysts on hematite, we decided to determine their onset potentials using $1~\mathrm{mV~s}^{-1}$ by cyclic voltammetry (see below), and also to verify that the currents near the onset potential were stable with potentiostatic experiments.
The $\mathrm{FeNiO_{x}}$ catalyst was deposited by linear sweep voltammetry between 1.0 and $1.2{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs. RHE under simulated AM 1.5 illumination $(100\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}},$ ). The deposition of catalyst is independent of front or back illumination. The loading after 50 linear sweeps was estimated to be $1.5{-2}~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ from the increase in absorbance (decrease in transmittance) after the deposition of the catalyst on $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ . Like on FTO, the $\mathrm{FeNiO_{x}}$ catalyst was nearly optically transparent on hematite, confirmed by the optical image and transmittance spectra (Figure 4a and 4b). SEM images revealed the conformal nature of the catalyst (Figure 4a) and XPS measurements confirmed the deposition of $\mathrm{FeNiO_{x}}$ , with a Fe:Ni ratio of approximately 70:30 (Figure S7). Upon front illumination, the $\mathrm{FeNiO_{x}}$ catalyst gave an improvement of more than $200~\mathrm{mV}$ for the onset potential of OER (Figure 4c).
Recently, Berlinguette and co-workers reported a novel method to deposit amorphous $\mathrm{NiFeO_{x}}$ catalyst by photodecomposition of a metal-organic precursor.10 This catalyst was applied by the group of Wang for OER on hematite where a significant reduction of onset potential was observed. The thickness of the $\mathrm{NiFeO_{x}}$ catalyst is on the order of $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ by this method, so the catalyst absorbs a significant portion of light.  Therefore, light transmittance is low and only back illumination for $\mathrm{NiFeO_{x}}$ -coated hematite is possible, which makes this catalyst unsuitable for a stacked tandem cell.  Nevertheless, we decided to compare our $\mathrm{FeNiO_{x}}$ catalyst under front illumination with the $\mathrm{NiFeO_{x}}$ catalyst under back illumination, using the slow scanning method (see above). Although the two catalysts gave a similar improved photocurrent at 1.2 V vs RHE, the onset of PEC OER took place at lower overpotential with $\mathrm{FeNiO_{x}}$ catalyst (Figure S11b). Furthermore, the onset of OER current on $\mathrm{NiFeO_{\mathrm{{x}}}}$ –coated hematite is masked by the oxidation of the catalyst, as evidenced by the significant reduction current of the catalyst on the return, cathodic scan. In short, the newly developed $\mathrm{FeNiO_{x}}$ catalyst also outperformed the best reported $\mathrm{NiFeO_{x}}$ catalyst on hematite in terms of net catalytic performance at low applied bias potential.
The roles of redox active oxide species in PEC OER by hematite are subject to debate in the literature and are perhaps dependent on the nature of the oxide species and the deposition methods. While the elucidation of the role of $\mathrm{FeNiO_{x}}$ catalyst is the subject of a follow up study, some preliminary data is presented here. The flat band potential of hematite thin films measured by Mott-Schottky analysis did not change after the deposition of $\mathrm{FeNiO_{x}}$ and was between 0.5 and $0.6\mathrm{\V}$ vs RHE under illumination (Figure S12). Likewise, the open-circuit photovoltages of bare hematite and hematite coated with $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ or $\mathrm{FeNiO_{x}}$ are all between 0.5 and $0.6\mathrm{~V~}$ (Figure S9).The effect of $\mathrm{FeNiO_{x}}$ was further probed using electrochemical impedance spectroscopy. An impedance model proposed by Klahr et al. was used to fit the impedance data (Figure S13a).18 The total resistance determined from the fitting parameters $\mathrm{(R_{impedance}=R_{s}+R_{c t}+R_{2}\Omega_{\rho}}$ ) agrees with the resistance measured as the derivative of the current potential curve, $\mathrm{\sfR}_{\mathrm{measured}}$ (Figure S13b). Here, ${\bf R}_{\mathrm{s}}$ is the solution resistance, $\mathrm{\bfR}_{\mathrm{ct}}$ is the resistance
